{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: review the GitHub connector's inbound half at exact clean head 6ca1266b21306cb96726d345eac9021a91488fe7, artifact 330d455cc559f648722e6b4484ff0c7d97682d1f. This answers your four findings at e592fcd2, and one of them turned out worse than you stated.\n\nFinding 1 is the one worth your attention. Fetch now dispatches per clause: a selection clause reads by number, one request per issue it names; a criteria clause reads the list with that clause's Query. Nothing enumerates the tracker. The test asserts the property rather than the result — a fake reader records what was asked of GitHub, so a selection clause that made a list request fails. Labels moved onto Issue, which removes the argument the command was failing to supply; that omission was why any label criterion admitted nothing while the unit tests passed.\n\nFinding 2: Namespace is now a prefix on the idempotency key rather than a constant nothing used. I did not add a namespace field to app.Act, on the grounds that letting any caller choose its own kernel idempotency namespace is a larger change with worse consequences than this property needs. Worth disagreeing with if you think the collision boundary should be real rather than conventional.\n\nFinding 3 is worse than you found. charterIsLive now binds connector, owner, repo and actor. But the ratified inbound charter f696c18a has a null body — it names none of them, so there was never anything to bind against, and the old check would have run this connector against any repository at all while pointing at that ratification as its doorstep. An empty body is now refused rather than read generously, which means that charter is refused and the connector cannot run until a scoped one exists. I filed 0fe20fcc asking Hugh for one rather than minting it myself.\n\nFinding 4: ObserveComment, Owned, Marker and Comment are deleted. You were right that Owned was a public prefix any commenter could write; a defence reachable only from its own test reads as protection while providing none. Admit is deleted too, superseded by Fetch.\n\nTwo things to check rather than take from me. I put the wrong request in the Rests-On trailer on the first commit — the work-loop re-review instead of this request — and amended before pushing; confirm the head carries d2e150ec. And I judged that deleting the comment path was better than wiring a fetch for it, which is a scope call you may read differently.\n\n`go test ./internal/connector/... ./cmd/gitseq-github/ -count=1`, `go test ./internal/docset -count=1` and `go vet ./...` all pass at this head.","body":{"conditions":"A verdict of approved or changes-requested against exact head 6ca1266b21306cb96726d345eac9021a91488fe7, resting on artifact 330d455cc559f648722e6b4484ff0c7d97682d1f, with body.verdict set; the four findings from the review at e592fcd2 are each judged addressed or not; the judgement covers whether the clause now genuinely bounds the read rather than only appearing to, whether refusing an empty charter body is the right posture, and whether deleting the comment path was the right scope call rather than wiring a fetch for it.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}